00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:42.228
I've had to operate, so the more
thoughtful you are about market trends

00:15:42.228 --> 00:15:46.480
that are really directly relevant to
you put them in a bigger picture.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:50.329
The more that comes across without
you ever having to say, hey,

00:15:50.329 --> 00:15:54.335
we know the market, you're just
showing people that you know it.

00:15:54.335 --> 00:15:58.020
And again,
there's a huge unfair advantage.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:02.019
Going back to my point before that
people companies have in African and

00:16:02.019 --> 00:16:05.020
Middle East,
there's almost no market research.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:10.930
You can have your own expression
definition of the market.

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:15.579
And frankly, that if you want
a huge body blow for visibility,

00:16:15.579 --> 00:16:19.900
I mean, that is a nobody in
any other part of the world.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:25.081
Would ever be able to do their
own primary market research and

00:16:25.081 --> 00:16:28.940
get it out there, except in Africa.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:33.024
So there's huge opportunities, which
is a quid pro quo for all the friction.

00:16:33.024 --> 00:16:38.768
[MUSIC]

00:16:38.768 --> 00:16:42.888
&gt;&gt; Going back to your earlier point, which
is companies don't really particularly in

00:16:42.888 --> 00:16:45.906
these emerging markets are not
really preparing to sell, but

00:16:45.906 --> 00:16:47.930
they should be prepared to be bought.

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:52.750
What things should I be thinking about
as a founder or CEO to make my company

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:58.110
attractive in the long run when
somebody starts to look under the hood.

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:00.830
&gt;&gt; If I'm a large company
doing an acquisition,

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:05.390
one of the things I want is a really
sturdy and deeper management team.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:10.489
So I'm going to have a premium in
my mind for second line management.

00:17:10.489 --> 00:17:12.682
Below the top two or three people,

00:17:12.682 --> 00:17:16.730
and I want to know that those
people are also able to scale.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:21.740
And in Africa, there's, as I said,
a huge dearth of that contrary.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:26.573
So at an early stage, companies have
the opportunity to create their own

00:17:26.573 --> 00:17:29.270
kind of talent academy internally.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:34.229
So growing your own, but being able to
show a buyer that you've got a full house.

00:17:34.229 --> 00:17:38.940
And when you're gone, there's three
other people who want your job.

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:42.970
That is incredibly
distinctive hard to do but

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:47.870
creates a degree of
resilience immediately.

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:51.802
So that kind of thing goes
an enormous way towards converting

00:17:51.802 --> 00:17:54.510
the buyer because it takes away the risk.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:57.160
[MUSIC]

00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:01.726
&gt;&gt; So, to sum it up you may be
undervaluing the human capital

00:18:01.726 --> 00:18:03.832
assets in your business.

00:18:03.832 --> 00:18:08.906
&gt;&gt; That's right,
a second thing that companies worry about

00:18:08.906 --> 00:18:14.710
from a buyer point of view is,
is it an African platform or not?

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:16.900
That's different for different sectors.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.353
What is good enough
international expansion, but

00:18:20.353 --> 00:18:23.140
the valuation is 50% higher.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:28.552
If you're an African platform, maybe
even 100% in some cases, versus the same

00:18:28.552 --> 00:18:33.490
amount of revenue that is regionally
locked in one or two markets only.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:37.677
We're actually working on a deal now
which is sort of somewhere between

00:18:37.677 --> 00:18:41.870
an acquisition and a merger
companies in two markets in Africa.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:44.387
And one of the main drivers for
this is for

00:18:44.387 --> 00:18:48.550
them to be able to operate under
regulation in both areas, right?

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:51.730
Rather than setting up in a new market and
going through all of that.

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:56.477
They want to do an acquisition in order
to be able to show immediately that they

00:18:56.477 --> 00:19:00.140
are a Pan African business
despite the regulation.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:04.026
And they think that should increase
their price per share almost overnight

00:19:04.026 --> 00:19:05.330
pretty significantly.

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:09.260
And everybody looks for one plus one
equals more than two being able to

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.850
do that is one very clear
expression of that.

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:18.275
&gt;&gt; I'd like to pivot to some sort
of more basic practical type advice

00:19:18.275 --> 00:19:23.970
from you just to help our listeners
understand a typical M&amp;A process.

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:28.129
And so like what are the typical
steps in the process?

00:19:28.129 --> 00:19:33.787
&gt;&gt; So at its heart, it's a deal so the two
elements of it are obviously finding or

00:19:33.787 --> 00:19:37.820
engaging a buyer who's serious and
then closing it.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:44.320
There are two very distinct parts to
the process getting a buyer on the hook.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:48.670
If I could put it that way, there
are many ways of going about doing it.

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:51.950
As I said,
if one wants to be bought not sold and

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:55.665
really what that means is
you want the phone to ring.

00:19:55.665 --> 00:20:00.939
So there are a million things that go into
the phone ringing confidence that you've

00:20:00.939 --> 00:20:06.080
built relationships with people,
you've encouraged a commercial deal.

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.500
You've informed their
own strategic thinking.

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:13.990
We've had a client who wrote a deck, we
encourage them to send it in PowerPoint,

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:14.528
not PDF.

00:20:14.528 --> 00:20:18.429
So that the buyer could lift by the way,
it was in the buyers colors,

00:20:18.429 --> 00:20:21.439
buyer could copy and
paste a bunch of the graphics for

00:20:21.439 --> 00:20:25.150
their own internal presentation
of what would make sense to do.

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:27.601
There's a lot of guidance informing, etc.

00:20:27.601 --> 00:20:31.139
When a buyer comes to the table
decide to do something.

00:20:31.139 --> 00:20:33.599
[MUSIC]

00:20:33.599 --> 00:20:36.960
&gt;&gt; So
I got the phone call the first phone call.

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:38.160
What do I do next, right?

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.351
I've been setting myself up,
I put myself out there,

00:20:40.351 --> 00:20:41.980
I've invested a corporate marketing.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:46.279
I'm a recognized thought leader,
I have some important sub contracts,

00:20:46.279 --> 00:20:47.630
showing traction.

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:51.880
I get the call, what do I need to know
about this person who's calling me?

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:54.700
How do I vet this potential buyer?

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.110
&gt;&gt; Well, it starts with what do you say?

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:57.850
What does a buyer want to hear?

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:01.030
On the one hand,
they want to hear that you're open, but

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:03.760
they don't expect to
hear any more than that.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:08.290
So the answer usually is,
something we've been thinking about.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:11.931
We've had on the radar screen our
investors, have been involved for

00:21:11.931 --> 00:21:14.210
x period of time, we're open to talking.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:17.230
It's not something that
we are planning to do.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:21.793
You want to sometimes give a little bit of
a feel of exclusivity to the sense of like

00:21:21.793 --> 00:21:24.258
we're not really going to
shop the company.

00:21:24.258 --> 00:21:27.612
Probably we're only going to do
a deal with somebody we know and

00:21:27.612 --> 00:21:29.810
actually your timing may not be bad.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.759
And what a buyers really worried about is
that the CEO in particular has invested so

00:21:34.759 --> 00:21:36.706
much of his or her time in blood.

00:21:36.706 --> 00:21:39.337
And guts in the business
that they couldn't,

00:21:39.337 --> 00:21:42.784
they just don't couldn't
imagine selling it to somebody.

00:21:42.784 --> 00:21:47.525
And a lot of deals, never got off
the ground because the buyer has a feeling

00:21:47.525 --> 00:21:49.989
that they're pushing water up hill.

00:21:49.989 --> 00:21:53.875
&gt;&gt; I don't want to lose that point where
the person who gets the call maybe

00:21:53.875 --> 00:21:58.320
actually really isn't emotionally
ready to sell their business.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:04.660
&gt;&gt; You always behave as if you're open
even if you're not emotionally ready.

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:06.520
You need to be a grown
up to run a company.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:09.680
You need to be a grown
up on these phone calls.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:14.529
You know the person calling has
got a strategy behind their call,

00:22:14.529 --> 00:22:17.010
has got money behind their call.

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:20.990
And you also don't know
what they're going to do.

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:24.775
I mean, they may be saying,
well do I acquire or

00:22:24.775 --> 00:22:28.100
do I actually invest to go in this market?

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:33.085
There's a lot of things you don't know,
and it would be the height of arrogance,

00:22:33.085 --> 00:22:36.620
that there's not a call
I really want to have.

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:40.335
If you're the CEO of a business
that's got traction,

00:22:40.335 --> 00:22:43.730
part of your job description
is workout an exit.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:44.730
That's your job.

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:49.515
So, it's as simple as do
that part of your job.

00:22:49.515 --> 00:22:54.739
[MUSIC]

00:22:54.739 --> 00:22:59.213
&gt;&gt; So what questions should the founder
be asking themselves in their quiet

00:22:59.213 --> 00:23:04.580
dialogue with themselves to understand
whether they are mentally ready.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:07.550
&gt;&gt; So
they're usually their own worst enemy.

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:10.850
And especially when you have
ecosystems like in Africa,

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:13.542
where hardly anybody's ever done, an exit.

00:23:13.542 --> 00:23:18.611
The playbook isn't been written, and
you don't know anybody who's done this so

00:23:18.611 --> 00:23:22.000
it's not like a valley where
you can call five people.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:28.440
And hey how does this all work, your first
job is to work out a, are they for real?

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:32.890
B, is it something that I
should spend time on now?

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:37.620
This is an example of where emotion and
reaction in a vacuum,

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:44.160
because it is a vacuum as an early
ecosystem, really can create a distortion.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.860
&gt;&gt; Let's go to that point,
so I got the call.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:49.100
There's no playbook online for my market.

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:50.360
Where do I go for help?

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:51.684
Who do I talk to?

00:23:51.684 --> 00:23:55.824
Where do I get, what resources do I,
yeah, I can't afford Victor Boston.

00:23:55.824 --> 00:23:57.412
Who do I call?

00:23:57.412 --> 00:24:01.714
&gt;&gt; You're touching a nerve a little
bit because this is really just

00:24:01.714 --> 00:24:06.253
where the embryonic nature of
an ecosystem really, really bites,

00:24:06.253 --> 00:24:08.340
right, in moments like this.

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:14.561
if you're the CEO, you have to be cautious
with who you talk to in your entire orbit.

00:24:14.561 --> 00:24:17.562
Ordinarily, you'd bring your two or

00:24:17.562 --> 00:24:22.268
three direct reports into the know,
relatively early on.

00:24:22.268 --> 00:24:25.812
And you'd kind of consult with them and
they would give you their view, and

00:24:25.812 --> 00:24:28.243
you want to make sure that it's good for
employees.

00:24:28.243 --> 00:24:34.128
I don't want to over exaggerate this,
but you do need to be careful.

00:24:34.128 --> 00:24:36.489
So that's a really lonely place to be.

00:24:36.489 --> 00:24:42.388
What's going to end up happening is that
the CEO, he or she needs to fly solo,

00:24:42.388 --> 00:24:47.648
for probably much longer than they
would do in a developed market.

00:24:47.648 --> 00:24:49.785
[MUSIC]

00:24:49.785 --> 00:24:56.687
&gt;&gt; Let's talk a little bit about
what happens as we close this deal.

00:24:56.687 --> 00:24:59.545
I'm the founder CEO,
my firm is being acquired.

00:24:59.545 --> 00:25:02.715
How should I think about my
role in this combined firm?

00:25:02.715 --> 00:25:05.541
What are some of the different
arrangements you've seen?

00:25:05.541 --> 00:25:10.957
&gt;&gt; So as a general rule,
large buyers want to legislate to

00:25:10.957 --> 00:25:17.670
keep the CEO much longer than they
end up needing him or her for.

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:18.694
&gt;&gt; Why is that?

00:25:18.694 --> 00:25:23.395
It's just a safety, derisk, he or
she has led this company and

00:25:23.395 --> 00:25:26.157
we want them to lead a business unit.

00:25:26.157 --> 00:25:28.140
And we want everybody in place.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:30.048
And by the way, if that person stays,

00:25:30.048 --> 00:25:32.565
we know that the other 30
people are going to stay.

00:25:32.565 --> 00:25:36.645
So we want the CEO to sign a three
year employment agreement or

00:25:36.645 --> 00:25:39.848
lockup or
whatever you want to call it, right?

00:25:39.848 --> 00:25:43.232
We want to put some of the money
they're going to get frankly

00:25:43.232 --> 00:25:46.050
at risk based on them
staying in the business.

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:50.765
More than 50% of the time, they all
realize after a year or a year and a half,

00:25:50.765 --> 00:25:53.773
business has moved on,
we got our arms around it.

00:25:53.773 --> 00:25:56.406
By the way,
you're really an entrepreneur at heart.

00:25:56.406 --> 00:25:58.822
I don't know if this business
unit thing is really for you,

00:25:58.822 --> 00:25:59.823
&gt;&gt; They accelerate it, and

00:25:59.823 --> 00:26:00.590
they buy them out.

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:05.712
Yeah, buy them out or terminate early,
or maybe change their role into some

00:26:05.712 --> 00:26:10.997
other role because sometimes there's
a bigger opportunity for them to be able

00:26:10.997 --> 00:26:16.385
to graduate to which is all fine, but far
less often than CEOs think is the case.

00:26:16.385 --> 00:26:21.171
Their role changes materially within
a couple of years of a deal, but

00:26:21.171 --> 00:26:24.469
the legislation at the time
you do the deal is for

00:26:24.469 --> 00:26:27.702
everything to be hardwired
exactly the same.

00:26:27.702 --> 00:26:32.337
As far as the second line people,
normally, there is more opportunity

00:26:32.337 --> 00:26:37.064
normally for those people than
the company would ever have standalone.

00:26:37.064 --> 00:26:39.791
I know it sounds a little
counter intuitive.

00:26:39.791 --> 00:26:45.700
But when you're looking at strategic
buyers coming into frontier Geography like

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:51.536
Africa, very few have got a big footprint
that you need to fold the company into.

00:26:51.536 --> 00:26:56.157
This is not about smashing this with that,
they're trying to build around it.

00:26:56.157 --> 00:26:59.691
They're trying to buy
Africa in many cases.

00:26:59.691 --> 00:27:04.384
Therefore, for the second line
person who's in their thirties and

00:27:04.384 --> 00:27:08.747
wants to be a CEO, you're just
going to get another huge addition

00:27:08.747 --> 00:27:11.483
to the resources you have to play with.

00:27:11.483 --> 00:27:14.464
And by the way,
somebody who's made a commitment,

00:27:14.464 --> 00:27:17.324
paid a lot of money,
to actually go win in Africa.

00:27:17.324 --> 00:27:21.723
So instead of being a startup
growth company, whatever,

00:27:21.723 --> 00:27:24.432
you're now seriously incumbent.

00:27:24.432 --> 00:27:27.301
Now, you may not want to do that forever
and want to start your own business.

00:27:27.301 --> 00:27:31.321
But my God, if you can do five,
seven years of that and really build it to

00:27:31.321 --> 00:27:35.745
scale, you can write your ticket anywhere
on the continent with any company.

00:27:35.745 --> 00:27:42.088
So that opportunity for
second line managers can really open up.

00:27:42.088 --> 00:27:47.041
&gt;&gt; What are some of the common mistakes
the CEOs make on the sell side

00:27:47.041 --> 00:27:48.701
in the M&amp;A process.

00:27:48.701 --> 00:27:52.927
&gt;&gt; Not rehearsing their full team,
number one.

00:27:52.927 --> 00:27:55.657
There was a deal we worked
on a few years ago.

00:27:55.657 --> 00:28:02.741
The buyer came in to do due diligence,
a day was on technical due diligence.

00:28:02.741 --> 00:28:05.846
They went through the whole day and
were perfectly satisfied.

00:28:05.846 --> 00:28:10.355
The CTO turns to the buyer and says,
I don't know why you bothered asking all

00:28:10.355 --> 00:28:14.462
these questions, you really should
have asked about blank, oops.

00:28:14.462 --> 00:28:18.088
Needless to say, technical due
diligence took weeks and weeks.

00:28:18.088 --> 00:28:20.968
And I think it turned out,
if I remember right,

00:28:20.968 --> 00:28:24.712
it was an open source licensing issue or
something like that.

00:28:24.712 --> 00:28:27.618
I can't remember what it was,
but you get the idea.

00:28:27.618 --> 00:28:30.990
So we rehearse and
rehearse the senior team on message.

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:35.839
So when you're doing due diligence,
which is after you've agreed

00:28:35.839 --> 00:28:40.444
the terms of the deal and
you're now trying to close it, right?

00:28:40.444 --> 00:28:45.156
The guidances, value can shift 20,
25% easily from

00:28:45.156 --> 00:28:49.868
what you thought you agreed,
and people don't realize,

00:28:49.868 --> 00:28:55.755
think due diligence is just checking
stuff and then we're going to close.

00:28:55.755 --> 00:29:00.935
Value shifts in all sorts of ways.

00:29:00.935 --> 00:29:05.729
&gt;&gt; So Victor, at the end of the sales
process, what does success look like?

00:29:05.729 --> 00:29:11.760
&gt;&gt; Success is having made only
small compromises late in the day.

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:17.374
Now, it's a kind of high level thing,
but you end up giving stuff away.

00:29:17.374 --> 00:29:21.404
Your maximum point of leverage when you're
selling a business is of the time you're

00:29:21.404 --> 00:29:22.777
agreeing the deal, right?

00:29:22.777 --> 00:29:26.969
At that point, your leverage begins
to wane away, until a week or

00:29:26.969 --> 00:29:31.486
two before the deal closes when you
have the least leverage possible.

00:29:31.486 --> 00:29:36.993
Success is that you live to fight
another day, you make small compromises.

00:29:36.993 --> 00:29:39.538
Actually what I try and
do is plan ahead for

00:29:39.538 --> 00:29:42.754
compromises that seem bigger
than they really are.

00:29:42.754 --> 00:29:46.154
So one big thing is around
the point we talked about before,

00:29:46.154 --> 00:29:49.297
which is management retention and
packages, right?

00:29:49.297 --> 00:29:52.425
If you can get a buyer
a little more comfortable,

00:29:52.425 --> 00:29:56.925
change things around a little bit so
that, that part is really solid,

00:29:56.925 --> 00:30:01.829
they might forget the $20 million they
want to take off of the price here.

00:30:01.829 --> 00:30:05.561
&gt;&gt; And you as the founder, have to
understand what's your zone of agreement?

00:30:05.561 --> 00:30:07.940
What are you actually willing to give up?

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:10.357
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
also you go a little further even.

00:30:10.357 --> 00:30:14.039
What you do is,
you then in the process of due diligence,

00:30:14.039 --> 00:30:17.586
you are already listening to
what the buyer is saying.

00:30:17.586 --> 00:30:21.086
And you get to understand what
really is important to the buyer.

00:30:21.086 --> 00:30:24.044
And so if you're already preparing two or
three or

00:30:24.044 --> 00:30:27.926
four of these gives late in the process,
you want to design them.

00:30:27.926 --> 00:30:32.882
So they're of most value to the buyer,
but it's based on listening to them.

00:30:32.882 --> 00:30:34.247
&gt;&gt; What do you wish I'd asked you,

00:30:34.247 --> 00:30:36.430
what should I have asked
you that I haven't asked?

00:30:36.430 --> 00:30:41.054
&gt;&gt; Why on earth would anybody start
a growth company in a place like Africa.

00:30:41.054 --> 00:30:44.833
The thing is, it's not as simple
as making a difference or

00:30:44.833 --> 00:30:49.325
having an impact or whatever,
I think it's more nuanced than that.

00:30:49.325 --> 00:30:54.558
We all in our lives look for
opportunities where we can really

00:30:54.558 --> 00:30:59.797
get a multiplier on our own time and
the value of our own time.

00:30:59.797 --> 00:31:05.633
And in a geography like the African
continent, the multiplier of value,

00:31:05.633 --> 00:31:10.528
multiplication on your own time
of entrepreneurial effort,

00:31:10.528 --> 00:31:15.723
you cannot get a greater multiplier
anywhere in the world today.

00:31:15.723 --> 00:31:20.338
The reality is that there's so
few companies doing it at scale.

00:31:20.338 --> 00:31:21.887
There's so much to be done.

00:31:21.887 --> 00:31:23.493
It isn't talked about,

00:31:23.493 --> 00:31:27.550
even from species point of view
frankly could move the needle.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:30.960
This is not about, well,
I want to feel good or give back or

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:35.098
make the world a better place,
nothing against any of that, right?

00:31:35.098 --> 00:31:39.018
But you through the sheer
effort that these CEOs and

00:31:39.018 --> 00:31:42.221
senior teams make in these geographies.

00:31:42.221 --> 00:31:47.100
They get such a higher multiplier on
the value of their time than anybody

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.510
ever could anywhere else on earth.

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:51.957
Why wouldn't you?

00:31:51.957 --> 00:31:54.984
&gt;&gt; Thank you Victor for guiding our
listeners through some of the best

00:31:54.984 --> 00:31:58.499
practices in the acquisition process,
but especially for your optimism and

00:31:58.499 --> 00:32:01.000
commitment to economic
prosperity on the continent.

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.919
This has been a masterclass from Grit &amp;
Growth with Stanford Graduate School of

00:32:04.919 --> 00:32:07.385
Business, and I'm your host,
Darrius Teeter.

